WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.911
This briefing comes from Book 07 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.911 --> 00:00:10.178
PKI and Certificates: the move from
Active Directory Certificate Services to

3
00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:15.687
Microsoft Cloud PKI, Entra certificate-based
authentication and the Federal PKI.

4
00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:19.953
Every other migration changes
which system answers the question

5
00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:23.163
"who is this?" Certificates are
how that answer gets proved.

6
00:00:23.163 --> 00:00:28.091
I'll cover why certificates outlive every
migration, what the target PKI is, who

7
00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:29.911
owns the certificate estate,

8
00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:33.900
and how to move it without
stranding a single certificate.

9
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.450
Why do certificates
outlive every migration?

10
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.613
Because a certificate isn't
a record in a directory.

11
00:00:40.613 --> 00:00:43.833
It's a signed statement that's
already been handed out:

12
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:47.126
the holder of this private
key is entitled to this name.

13
00:00:47.126 --> 00:00:50.237
When the directory moves,
the certificate doesn't.

14
00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:54.612
It keeps working until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

15
00:00:54.612 --> 00:00:56.350
A great deal relies on it.

16
00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.325
Domain controllers need
KDC certificates and LDAPS.

17
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:04.432
Smart card and PIV logon need
the issuing CA in the forest's

18
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:08.625
NTAuth store and a certificate
on every domain controller.

19
00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:13.800
802.1X and VPN need NPS
and device certificates.

20
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.340
TLS listeners, including
SQL Server, need them,

21
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.600
and so do code signing
and encryption keys.

22
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.650
That gives the program two clocks.

23
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.870
On the directory's clock, cutover
is declared complete and the

24
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.338
first expiry wave finds no issuer,
months later, far from its cause.

25
00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:35.129
On the certificate's clock,
the replacement issuer is

26
00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:38.749
live before the first expiry,
and AD CS retires last.

27
00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:44.090
So the estate outlives the directory twice
over: issued certificates run until they

28
00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.490
expire, and the forest needs an issuer
until its last domain controller retires.

29
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.602
A certificate estate used to be something
you could leave alone between renewals.

30
00:01:54.602 --> 00:01:56.290
Three changes have ended that.

31
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.565
First, public TLS lifetimes are
shrinking on a fixed schedule.

32
00:02:00.565 --> 00:02:07.903
Under the CA/Browser Forum's ballot
SC-081v3, adopted in April 2025,

33
00:02:07.903 --> 00:02:15.377
the maximum validity fell from 398
days to 200 days on March 15, 2026.

34
00:02:15.377 --> 00:02:23.566
It drops to 100 days on March 15,
2027, and to 47 days on March 15, 2029.

35
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:28.066
On the book's arithmetic, a thousand
certificates means about seven

36
00:02:28.066 --> 00:02:32.165
renewals every working day now,
and about thirty-one at 47 days.

37
00:02:32.165 --> 00:02:36.315
Second, mapping rules changed
under certificates already in use.

38
00:02:36.315 --> 00:02:43.731
Under KB5014754, domain controllers
moved to Full Enforcement on February 11,

39
00:02:43.731 --> 00:02:48.865
2025, with no way back after
the September 9, 2025 update.

40
00:02:48.865 --> 00:02:51.477
Third, the algorithms have a horizon.

41
00:02:51.477 --> 00:02:56.523
NIST's draft transition plan
would deprecate RSA-2048

42
00:02:56.523 --> 00:03:02.128
after 2030 and disallow RSA and
ECDSA signatures after 2035.

43
00:03:02.128 --> 00:03:05.416
So manual lifecycle
management can't keep up,

44
00:03:05.416 --> 00:03:08.970
and the move off AD CS
runs as its own program.

45
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.770
What does AD CS hold up today?

46
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.133
It isn't one server.

47
00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:17.495
It's a hierarchy, usually an offline
root and enterprise issuing CAs;

48
00:03:17.495 --> 00:03:21.702
a set of forest-wide objects in
the Public Key Services container,

49
00:03:21.702 --> 00:03:26.844
including the templates and the NTAuth
store; and Group Policy autoenrollment,

50
00:03:26.844 --> 00:03:29.882
which runs on domain
members every eight hours.

51
00:03:29.882 --> 00:03:33.429
The inventory has four layers:
the CAs, the templates,

52
00:03:33.429 --> 00:03:37.470
the certificates actually
issued, and the relying parties.

53
00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:41.410
The relying parties set the order
of work, because a new issuer is

54
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.870
useful only once everything that
validates its certificates trusts it.

55
00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.558
Then every template and use
case gets one of four verdicts.

56
00:03:49.558 --> 00:03:55.333
MIGRATE, where Cloud PKI or Entra
certificate-based authentication replaces it.

57
00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:59.486
BRIDGE, where both issuers
run because the consumer

58
00:03:59.486 --> 00:04:03.833
depends on AD or a server:
domain controllers, LDAPS, NPS.

59
00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:08.558
RETAIN, where there's no cloud
equivalent, like HSM-held code signing.

60
00:04:08.558 --> 00:04:11.395
And RETIRE, where the
consumer is going away.

61
00:04:11.395 --> 00:04:16.871
The book corrects its source here:
internal web and SQL Server TLS can't move

62
00:04:16.871 --> 00:04:22.620
to Cloud PKI, and S/MIME stays BRIDGE
because its key history has to survive.

63
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.621
AD CS keeps issuing trusted
credentials while the migration runs,

64
00:04:27.621 --> 00:04:32.645
so the CA you're leaving has to be the
best-defended system you own until it's gone.

65
00:04:32.645 --> 00:04:36.507
The book frames hardening strictly
as a detect-and-fix checklist.

66
00:04:36.507 --> 00:04:41.708
The eight classes carry the ESC labels
that Microsoft Defender for Identity also

67
00:04:41.708 --> 00:04:45.838
uses: requester-supplied subjects
on authentication templates;

68
00:04:45.838 --> 00:04:49.968
Any Purpose or no-EKU templates;
open enrollment agent templates;

69
00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:55.397
writable template permissions; writable
Public Key Services objects such as NTAuth;

70
00:04:55.397 --> 00:04:59.986
the CA-wide flag that lets any request
supply a subject alternative name;

71
00:04:59.986 --> 00:05:01.898
unprivileged CA permissions;

72
00:05:01.898 --> 00:05:05.645
and web enrollment without
HTTPS and Extended Protection.

73
00:05:05.645 --> 00:05:08.273
Defender for Identity
detects most of them,

74
00:05:08.273 --> 00:05:11.120
and the CA-level checks
need its sensor on the CA.

75
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.601
The fifth class has no assessment,

76
00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:18.032
so it's a manual review backed
by directory-change event 5136.

77
00:05:18.032 --> 00:05:20.119
The ratings are the program's own scoring.

78
00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:25.339
The gate is simple: every Critical and High
finding is fixed and verified before any

79
00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:30.558
workload migrates, because the recommended
cloud issuer chains to this hierarchy.

80
00:05:30.558 --> 00:05:33.757
And none of it has evidence
until CA auditing is on,

81
00:05:33.757 --> 00:05:36.390
which takes two settings together.

82
00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.415
So what is the target?

83
00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:41.777
Not Cloud PKI instead of AD CS.

84
00:05:41.777 --> 00:05:44.878
That's true for devices
and wrong for the estate.

85
00:05:44.878 --> 00:05:49.027
The target is four issuers, each
for the subjects only it can serve.

86
00:05:49.027 --> 00:05:55.503
Microsoft Cloud PKI issues to Intune-managed
devices, and only to them, through

87
00:05:55.503 --> 00:06:01.878
SCEP, with three CAs per tenant, a CRL
valid for seven days and no OCSP responder.

88
00:06:01.878 --> 00:06:08.251
A smaller, hardened AD CS stays for domain
controllers and LDAPS, NPS and RADIUS,

89
00:06:08.251 --> 00:06:14.428
internal server TLS, network devices
through NDES, and HSM-held code signing.

90
00:06:14.428 --> 00:06:18.869
The Federal PKI keeps issuing PIV;
the target's job is to trust it,

91
00:06:18.869 --> 00:06:23.739
by distributing the federal root
and uploading the chain to Entra ID.

92
00:06:23.739 --> 00:06:27.302
And a public CA serves names
the internet must trust.

93
00:06:27.302 --> 00:06:30.027
One decision ties them together: the root.

94
00:06:30.027 --> 00:06:35.459
For an established hierarchy, the
program recommends bring-your-own-CA,

95
00:06:35.459 --> 00:06:39.141
a Cloud PKI issuing CA
signed by the offline root.

96
00:06:39.141 --> 00:06:44.290
It keeps the existing chain, and makes
the root's CRL a production dependency.

97
00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:45.852
Check the entitlement too.

98
00:06:45.852 --> 00:06:51.241
The July 2026 change that puts
Cloud PKI in Microsoft 365 E5 and E7

99
00:06:51.241 --> 00:06:57.190
covers commercial customers, so
confirm it for a government tenant.

100
00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.527
Four issuers need one lifecycle platform.

101
00:07:00.527 --> 00:07:04.878
Intune and Cloud PKI renew what
they put on managed devices.

102
00:07:04.878 --> 00:07:09.578
Nothing Microsoft-native does that
for AD CS server certificates,

103
00:07:09.578 --> 00:07:13.852
public TLS, appliances, or
certificates nobody inventoried.

104
00:07:13.852 --> 00:07:17.275
That's the job of certificate
lifecycle management:

105
00:07:17.275 --> 00:07:21.253
discovery, ownership and
automated renewal across issuers.

106
00:07:21.253 --> 00:07:24.867
The program's platform is
CyberArk Certificate Manager,

107
00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:26.790
now part of Palo Alto Networks.

108
00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.951
It's the program's existing vendor,

109
00:07:28.951 --> 00:07:33.503
so the program consolidates there
and adds no second lifecycle vendor.

110
00:07:33.503 --> 00:07:38.589
The names have moved: Venafi became
part of CyberArk in October 2024,

111
00:07:38.589 --> 00:07:44.130
Palo Alto Networks completed its
acquisition of CyberArk on February 11,

112
00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:49.490
2026, and the SaaS edition is now sold
as Next-Generation Trust Security.

113
00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.428
Two facts shape the deployment.

114
00:07:51.428 --> 00:07:56.339
The FedRAMP Marketplace has no listing for
the certificate product, so the program

115
00:07:56.339 --> 00:08:00.965
runs Certificate Manager, Self-Hosted
inside its own authorization boundary.

116
00:08:00.965 --> 00:08:05.041
And its AD CS account holds the
certificate-manager permission,

117
00:08:05.041 --> 00:08:09.400
so it's scoped and reviewed like
any other certificate manager.

118
00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:11.962
Who owns the certificate estate?

119
00:08:11.962 --> 00:08:14.596
In most forests, whoever installed the CA.

120
00:08:14.596 --> 00:08:17.383
Domain Admins and Enterprise Admins are CA

121
00:08:17.383 --> 00:08:20.713
administrators by default,
and nobody decided that.

122
00:08:20.713 --> 00:08:22.950
The book separates deciding from doing.

123
00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:26.473
The sponsor and CIO decide
the target hierarchy,

124
00:08:26.473 --> 00:08:32.462
when each AD CS component retires, and who
owns renewal for each certificate class.

125
00:08:32.462 --> 00:08:35.642
The CISO and ISSO decide issuance policy,

126
00:08:35.642 --> 00:08:39.275
trust anchors and the
minimum binding strength.

127
00:08:39.275 --> 00:08:42.579
The authorizing official
accepts standing exceptions,

128
00:08:42.579 --> 00:08:44.337
such as a low-affinity binding.

129
00:08:44.337 --> 00:08:50.962
PKI and CA administrators run the CAs,
templates, CRLs and key ceremonies.

130
00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:56.530
The identity team owns the Entra trust store
and bindings, endpoint engineering owns

131
00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:01.862
the Intune profiles, and the CLM platform
owner runs discovery and server renewal.

132
00:09:01.862 --> 00:09:04.597
Server, network and application owners,

133
00:09:04.597 --> 00:09:08.326
the PIV office and security
operations are consulted.

134
00:09:08.326 --> 00:09:10.825
The principle is separation of duties.

135
00:09:10.825 --> 00:09:15.950
Whoever can change a CA must never be the
only person who decides what it issues.

136
00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.518
And whoever administers directory sync can

137
00:09:18.518 --> 00:09:21.580
change which account a
certificate signs in as.

138
00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:22.943
How.

139
00:09:22.943 --> 00:09:24.417
Start with devices.

140
00:09:24.417 --> 00:09:27.299
Intune delivers certificates
by three paths,

141
00:09:27.299 --> 00:09:31.318
and each needs the trusted
certificate profile deployed first.

142
00:09:31.318 --> 00:09:36.320
SCEP from Cloud PKI is the cleanest:
the device makes its own key pair,

143
00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.804
with no NDES server,
connector or on-premises CA.

144
00:09:39.804 --> 00:09:44.539
SCEP from AD CS runs through NDES
and the Certificate Connector;

145
00:09:44.539 --> 00:09:48.202
its template lets the
request supply the subject,

146
00:09:48.202 --> 00:09:52.579
so its Enroll permission stays
limited to the NDES account.

147
00:09:52.579 --> 00:09:56.542
PKCS makes the key on the
connector server and exports it,

148
00:09:56.542 --> 00:09:59.155
which is why the program prefers SCEP.

149
00:09:59.155 --> 00:10:01.742
Strong mapping is no longer optional.

150
00:10:01.742 --> 00:10:08.330
KB5014754 began in
compatibility mode in May 2022.

151
00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:14.161
Domain controllers moved to Full
Enforcement on February 11, 2025, and since

152
00:10:14.161 --> 00:10:19.992
the September 9, 2025 update the registry
key is ignored, so there's no way back.

153
00:10:19.992 --> 00:10:22.796
A certificate used against
a domain controller

154
00:10:22.796 --> 00:10:25.180
without a strong mapping is rejected now.

155
00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:30.491
So SCEP profiles add the on-premises
security identifier as a SAN URI, for user

156
00:10:30.491 --> 00:10:36.217
certificates on every platform and device
certificates on hybrid-joined Windows.

157
00:10:36.217 --> 00:10:41.110
Pilot it in a new profile, because
editing one revokes what it issued.

158
00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:46.647
On the user side, Entra certificate-based
authentication is a relying party.

159
00:10:46.647 --> 00:10:49.662
It validates certificates and issues none,

160
00:10:49.662 --> 00:10:54.435
so its risk is the opposite: a
certificate that should fail might pass.

161
00:10:54.435 --> 00:10:56.547
Trust and revocation first.

162
00:10:56.547 --> 00:11:01.061
Upload every CA in the chain
to the PKI-based trust store.

163
00:11:01.061 --> 00:11:03.109
Each CA gets one HTTP CRL,

164
00:11:03.109 --> 00:11:08.847
which Entra ID must download within
ten seconds or the sign-in fails.

165
00:11:08.847 --> 00:11:11.511
There's no OCSP and no LDAP.

166
00:11:11.511 --> 00:11:13.646
Turn on Require CRL validation,

167
00:11:13.646 --> 00:11:18.822
because a CA uploaded without a CRL
lets revoked certificates keep working.

168
00:11:18.822 --> 00:11:19.823
Then binding.

169
00:11:19.823 --> 00:11:21.573
Issuer and serial number,

170
00:11:21.573 --> 00:11:25.823
subject key identifier and
public-key hash are high affinity.

171
00:11:25.823 --> 00:11:29.985
Principal name, email, subject,
and issuer plus subject are low;

172
00:11:29.985 --> 00:11:33.086
the old guide called
issuer plus subject high.

173
00:11:33.086 --> 00:11:38.635
A certificate counts as multifactor only
when a PIN or biometric protects it:

174
00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:41.323
a PIV card, not a software certificate.

175
00:11:41.323 --> 00:11:45.560
And staged rollout is a temporary
test, limited to ten groups.

176
00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:48.062
Production requires high affinity,

177
00:11:48.062 --> 00:11:51.648
because CBA is only as strong
as its weakest binding.

178
00:11:51.648 --> 00:11:55.916
But switch only once every user's
certificate user IDs are right,

179
00:11:55.916 --> 00:11:58.210
or those users are locked out.

180
00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.422
For most of the migration,
both PKIs are production.

181
00:12:02.422 --> 00:12:08.048
Devices carry certificates from both issuers,
and relying parties trust both chains.

182
00:12:08.048 --> 00:12:10.597
Three coexistence rules carry the weight.

183
00:12:10.597 --> 00:12:12.960
Trust goes up before issuance moves.

184
00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:17.510
Nothing is revoked to force a move;
old certificates leave at renewal.

185
00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:22.411
And every CRL must be reachable from
inside the network, from the internet

186
00:12:22.411 --> 00:12:27.473
and from Entra ID, because most
coexistence outages are revocation outages.

187
00:12:27.473 --> 00:12:29.830
The migration runs in six gated waves,

188
00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:34.622
about forty-four weeks on the program's
planning figures for a single forest.

189
00:12:34.622 --> 00:12:37.487
Assess ends when every
template is classified

190
00:12:37.487 --> 00:12:39.984
and Critical and High findings are fixed.

191
00:12:39.984 --> 00:12:45.310
Foundation puts the cloud CA live, with
trust on ninety-nine percent of devices.

192
00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:48.585
Pilot needs ninety-five
percent issuance success.

193
00:12:48.585 --> 00:12:51.072
Scale moves every managed device.

194
00:12:51.072 --> 00:12:57.398
Cutover converts federated domains and
turns off AD FS certificate authentication.

195
00:12:57.398 --> 00:13:01.359
And Decommission retires what
served only migrated workloads.

196
00:13:01.359 --> 00:13:03.635
Every wave has a rehearsed rollback.

197
00:13:03.635 --> 00:13:08.004
Autoenrollment stays on through the
pilot, and AD FS stays restorable

198
00:13:08.004 --> 00:13:12.510
for thirty days after cutover, with
a four-hour recovery objective.

199
00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:16.535
Migration ends on a
date; certificates don't.

200
00:13:16.535 --> 00:13:21.150
With public TLS at 200 days today
and 47 days from March 2029,

201
00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:26.047
no certificate class may depend
on someone remembering a date.

202
00:13:26.047 --> 00:13:28.410
Three renewal engines cover the target.

203
00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:31.254
Intune profiles renew managed devices at

204
00:13:31.254 --> 00:13:34.098
the profile's threshold,
with a new key pair.

205
00:13:34.098 --> 00:13:37.781
AD CS autoenrollment
renews domain controllers,

206
00:13:37.781 --> 00:13:40.747
LDAPS and RADIUS while the forest stays.

207
00:13:40.747 --> 00:13:44.198
And CyberArk Certificate
Manager renews server,

208
00:13:44.198 --> 00:13:48.910
appliance and public TLS, using ACME
where the endpoint supports it.

209
00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:54.414
Monitoring watches signals, not
outages: expiry with no renewal path,

210
00:13:54.414 --> 00:13:58.760
a stale CRL, issuance outside
policy, trust-store drift.

211
00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:02.785
The checks run at least weekly,
and their history is the evidence.

212
00:14:02.785 --> 00:14:05.823
Retiring an AD CS CA comes last.

213
00:14:05.823 --> 00:14:07.560
Confirm nothing depends on it.

214
00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.622
Stop issuance, and back
up the database and key.

215
00:14:10.622 --> 00:14:15.511
Revoke what's left for cessation of
operation and publish a final CRL.

216
00:14:15.511 --> 00:14:19.110
Remove the role services
and clean every trust store.

217
00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:24.548
And keep the CRL and AIA endpoints serving
until the last certificate expires.

218
00:14:24.548 --> 00:14:29.230
Under bring-your-own-CA, the CA
that signed the cloud issuer stays.

219
00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.355
So, four decisions.

220
00:14:31.355 --> 00:14:35.837
Confirm the root decision: bring-your-own-CA,
signed by the offline root,

221
00:14:35.837 --> 00:14:39.005
once the hierarchy has
passed the hardening gate.

222
00:14:39.005 --> 00:14:42.762
Settle the Cloud PKI entitlement
for the government tenant,

223
00:14:42.762 --> 00:14:46.218
rather than assuming the
commercial E5 change applies.

224
00:14:46.218 --> 00:14:49.687
Stand up CyberArk Certificate
Manager, Self-Hosted,

225
00:14:49.687 --> 00:14:54.043
inside the authorization boundary,
as the single lifecycle tool.

226
00:14:54.043 --> 00:14:58.613
And name the owners: issuance policy
and trust anchors with the CISO,

227
00:14:58.613 --> 00:15:01.981
and one renewal owner for
every certificate class.

228
00:15:01.981 --> 00:15:07.683
The next step is wave one, Assess: four
weeks to inventory every CA and template,

229
00:15:07.683 --> 00:15:10.446
close the Critical and High findings,

230
00:15:10.446 --> 00:15:14.367
classify every use case, and
list every relying party.

231
00:15:14.367 --> 00:15:16.538
Run certificates on their own clock,

232
00:15:16.538 --> 00:15:19.793
and the old CAs can retire
without stranding anything.

233
00:15:19.793 --> 00:15:23.651
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07 has the detail,

234
00:15:23.651 --> 00:15:29.130
every claim footnoted, with deployment
checklists and a template-by-template matrix.

235
00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:29.950
Thank you.
